<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piedmont</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Piemonte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piedmont is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, enclosed on three sides by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> as its capital. From the 11th century the house of Savoy gained lands there, and Piedmont became the core of its state after the capital moved to Turin in 1563. As the heart of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, it led the unification of Italy in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piedmont.jpg" alt="Piedmont" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piedmont_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
